xnu 0151: find the QUP4 pad order by reading the product id

The 0150 sweep powered the part up and found that one of gpio16-19 drives its
line high against the pad's pull-down, so the part is alive on this bus and the
order this driver assumed (16 clk, 17 mosi, 18 miso, 19 cs) is wrong. A read of
0x00010014 is the test that cannot be faked: only a correct frame makes the
part answer "BERLIN" (42 45 52 4c 49 4e) at bytes 8-13.

Power the rails once, then try the 24 assignments of the four pads (one CS,
three for clk/mosi/miso), each after a 15 ms reset pulse and the part's 120 ms
boot time, writing the 0xAA boot-option handshake first as brl_dev_confirm
does. Stop at the assignment that returns the id and print the echo too.

